# What `docker build` is NOT allowed to see.
#
# The build context is the repo root for both images — they are workspace
# builds and need `bun.lock` plus every workspace manifest, which no
# subdirectory context could give them. That makes this file the only thing
# standing between a `docker build` and the whole working tree, so it is
# written as a denylist of things that are either secret, huge, or wrong.

# --- Secret ---
# The one entry that is not an optimisation. The root .env holds the real JWT
# secrets and the database password; compose passes what a container needs as
# environment variables at *run* time, and anything copied in at build time is
# readable forever by anyone who can pull the image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- Wrong, not just large ---
# Bun's isolated linker fills node_modules with relative symlinks into
# node_modules/.bun. Copying a host tree in would shadow the install the
# Dockerfile does, and on a different libc would ship native builds that cannot
# load. Every image runs its own `bun install`.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Prisma's client is generated for a target runtime. The image regenerates it
# from the schema rather than inheriting whatever the host last produced.
packages/database/generated

# Build output, likewise rebuilt inside the image.
apps/web/.next
apps/web/out
dist
build
*.tsbuildinfo

# Uploaded attachments — user data on a volume, never an image layer.
apps/api/storage

# --- Noise ---
.git
.gitignore
.github
.husky
.turbo
**/.turbo
.claude
**/.claude
.agents
**/.agents
.windsurf
**/.windsurf
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
*.log
coverage
*.lcov
README.md
**/README.md
**/AGENTS.md
**/CLAUDE.md

# Compose and deployment files. Nothing inside an image reads them, and listing
# them here means editing nginx.conf does not invalidate the build cache.
compose.yml
compose.prod.yml
infra
.dockerignore
Dockerfile
**/Dockerfile
